 method has its flaws  so-called scientists have been wrong in  the past and thus it's really difficult  to know with any degree of certainty  what the objective truth is today about  the impact of humans on the on the  climate what is being sort of misused or  perverted about the scientific method by  that assertion from climate sceptics  that's a great question very timely I  actually so the point is that science  when you talk about this sort of science  complex climate science for example you  have to use that  stakes right you don't have a simple  little law like Newton's law that tells  you exactly where the moon is going to  be you know three months from now you  have to use statistical inference right  and so you can never be a hundred  percent sure of a prediction but you can  say that you have incredible compelling  evidence that if all the observations  and all the calculations of 99 percent  of the scientists are telling us that  the world is warming up and you look at  the emissions you know from 1850 on and  you see the big elbow picking up you  know about 20 30 years ago that this is  happening and that statistically the  odds are overwhelming that this is  happening because humans have been  putting stuff you know in the  environment for 200 years quite  intensely then I would say that we  should pay attention you can't say you  you can't use that theory the climate  change for example theory to say that  you know there's gonna be a hurricane in  the Bahamas on the 28th of September and  2017 that is not that kind of theory  that is Newton's theory of gravity and  orbits and stuff but it's a theory that  is going to say that if the trend  continues the probability of having you  know more dramatic weather-related  events is going to increase with a  certain rate and everything that we have  seen so far points to this correctness  of that theory so what people the  mistake that people make is first of all  to politicize a scientific question  right because climate change has  political implications and economic  implications of course but its first and  foremost a question about how about  science you know what can we say about  climate modeling and the measure and  measurements were making about the world  and I always say that even if you are a  climate skeptic as you say um we should  all be very aware that the planet is a  finite it has a finite environment you  know there is only so much a planet  can do you know it's a big planet but it  is only so much a planet can do so there  is no loss certainly there are issues  with what kind of approaches are gonna  take from the coal industry of course  you won't like this but there's no loss  in trying to protect as much as you can  the environment and our atmosphere so to  go a little bit from the sort of right  and wrong of a scientific assertion to  the concept of moral or ethical  correctness sam harris has written the  moral landscape there's been a lot  written about whether or not science  rather than religion in many cases can  tell us what is morally or ethically  right or wrong what are some important  considerations when we evaluate Sciences  ability to tell us objectively whether  something is ethical or unethical moral  or immoral that's a question that if  people are going to try to use cognitive  neuroscience you know to find out where  morality sits I think that's just not  going to go anywhere there is a question  where you really need not just science  but you need any the humanities as well  so I always say that science is actually  a moral what is what what has morality  is how you choose to apply the  scientific knowledge right so I actually  just wrote something on NPR about this a  week ago is the science know right from  wrong and and to make a long story short  I was talking about I was talking about  the issue of the atomic bomb right and  the fact that it was deployed in a  civilian population who made that choice  right well it turns out that the  scientists had a some an agreement with  the state and this relationship between  science and state is a very complex and  fragile one because scientists need the  state in order to fund their research  and the state means the scientists in  order to advance their defense agenda  right but at the end of the day  the decision on how to use the  scientific developments comes from the  people in power and as we know from  history those people in power are not  the scientists right they are the people  in this active branch of government  mostly military so so the point is the  only solution here according to me  anyways is you have to be very aware of  who are the leaders that you're choosing  and who you're choosing to work for so  perhaps at the individual level the  scientist has the choice of aligning him  or herself with the leadership that he  or she chooses or working with the  corporation that he or she chooses  within a certain limit because people  have to eat they have to get jobs but  the point is that the moral choices come  not with the use of the scientists of  the science by the scientists but how  you choose to give what you know to the  people they actually have the power to  use what you know do you where do you  fall on the idea that maybe the the  concept of morality or ethics does lend  itself more to some of the subjectivity  of the humanities but that science can  very definitively measure for example  well-being right and well-being could be  defined as you know life expectancy or  the number of people who would be saved  versus the number of people who might  perish if a certain medication research  program is put forward talk a little bit  about that I mean what can and can't  science measure in an objective way well  there is no question that there's the  feel-good thing but you know there are  bad things that can make you feel good  as well right so it's it's it's really  very subtle to say that science can be  used as a measure of wellness you know I  think because you can you can be a drug  addict you know and that's very bad but  if you take Harry and you're gonna feel  better you know  and and and you're gonna sell these  endorphins you know system and so you're  using science in the right context here  are you just fooling yourself because  it's you know you see the endorphins  there right so I would be very very  careful when people start to push  science into these sorts of realms  because this is really not i believe  what science is about you know science  is about constructing a narrative of the  natural world us included right so we  try to make sense of nature we try to  make sense of our place in nature we try  to understand human consciousness and  when you start to talk about the values  of choices that we make right and you  can say that for a psychopath killing  people will make him or her feel good  you know so if you're going to put an  fMRI on the head of a psychopath if  during the act a violent act you're  gonna see cannabinoids and you're going  to see endorphins flooding in and I'm  gonna say hey the measurements indicate  that this person is feeling good doing  this even though what he or she is doing  is profoundly wrong right  so I would be very very careful with  that sort of let's use science to  conquer in a sense parts of the  humanities which are kind of iffy  because the humanities tend to be iffy  you know and I'm speaking this as a  theoretical physicist you know I think  what we need is to understand the need  for a complementarity of different kinds  of knowledge as you know and the  scientific and the humanities they and  the social scientists they all have  something to say when the question is  the choices that we make about what is  right and what is wrong absolutely  fascinating we've been speaking with  Marcelo gleiser theoretical physicist  author NPR commentator his most recent  books are the island of knowledge and  the simple beauty of the unexpected  thanks so much for talking to us about  these issues  my pleasure David thank you so much for  having me
 

